<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gamal Abdel Nasser</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1918">1918</a>–<a href="/years/1970">1970</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Nasser</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Gamal Abdel Nasser was an Egyptian army officer who led the Free Officers' coup of 1952 and became president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> in 1956. He nationalised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suez-canal">Suez Canal</a> in 1956, built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aswan-high-dam">Aswan High Dam</a> and pursued Arab socialism. The leading figure of pan-Arab nationalism, he headed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-arab-republic">United Arab Republic</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> from 1958 to 1961.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/gamal-abdel-nasser.jpg" alt="Gamal Abdel Nasser" width="320" height="419" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Stevan Kragujević · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stevan_Kragujevic,_Gamal_Abdel_Naser_u_Beogradu,_1962_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
